
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You created your own systemd unit files.<br>If you missed the article, you find it <a href="https://tomsitcafe.com/2026/09/10/systemd-create-your-own-unit-files/">here</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Now you learn to run it on a schedule &#8211; instead of starting it manually.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The systemd timer way.</p>



<span id="more-9404"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A systemd timer does not perform the task itself.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It activates a service.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>The service defines what should happen.</li>



<li>The timer defines when it should happen.</li>
</ul>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The Systemd Unit File</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You have a simple service.<br>It does not have to run as a daemon.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Imagine a backup script.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>unit file</strong> looks like this:</p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[Unit]</div><div class="cm-line">Description=Create a system backup</div><div class="cm-line"></div><div class="cm-line">[Service]</div><div class="cm-line">Type=oneshot</div><div class="cm-line">ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/system-backup.sh</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The service type is <code>oneshot</code>.<br>It runs the command and exits.<br>It does not need to run continuously.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The timer will start the service according to its schedule.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After creating or changing a unit file, tell systemd to reload its unit files:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl daemon-reload</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The service is defined in the <code>/etc/systemd/system/system-backup.service</code> unit file.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can trigger a manual run of the service:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl start system-backup.service</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Or you create a <strong>systemd timer</strong> to run it on a schedule.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The Systemd Timer</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add a new file &#8211; <code>/etc/systemd/system/system-backup.timer</code>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The timer has three sections.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>[Unit]</code> section describes the timer.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>[Timer]</code> section contains the schedule.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>[Install]</code> section determines how the timer is enabled.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The system-backup timer should look like this:</p>


<div style="--line-number-gutter-width: 1ch" class="show-line-numbers wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">[Unit]</div><div class="cm-line">Description=Run system backup daily</div><div class="cm-line"></div><div class="cm-line">[Timer]</div><div class="cm-line">OnCalendar=daily</div><div class="cm-line"></div><div class="cm-line">[Install]</div><div class="cm-line">WantedBy=timers.target</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">From now on, you start the timer instead of starting the service manually.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The timer will activate the service according to its schedule.</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">system-backup.timer</div><div class="cm-line">        │</div><div class="cm-line">        │ activates</div><div class="cm-line">        ▼</div><div class="cm-line">system-backup.service</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You activate the timer with:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl daemon-reload</div><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl enable --now system-backup.timer</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>enable</code> command makes the timer start automatically when the system boots.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>--now</code> option starts the timer immediately as well.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Now the files are:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">/etc/systemd/system/</div><div class="cm-line">├── system-backup.service</div><div class="cm-line">└── system-backup.timer</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The schedule can be configured precisely, for example:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">OnCalendar=*-*-* 03:00:00</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">More information can be found on the <code>systemd.timer</code> manual page.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Check The Timers</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can list the timers:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl list-timers</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Check a specific timer:</p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo systemctl list-timers system-backup.timer</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Systemd provides an integrated system for managing the scheduling.<br>The configuration of units and timers live in the same directory.<br>The format is similar.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you already know cron, think of a systemd timer as the scheduling mechanism &#8211;<br>but remember that systemd separates the schedule from the task itself.</p>
